"""NCERT Class 3 Mathematics - Chapter 10 Part 2"""

slides_part2 = [{'id': 9, 'title': 'Metre Rope Knots', 'subtitle': 'Crafting an accurate portable measuring rope using standardized reference rods', 'speech': 'To measure long distances and large objects around the school without carrying a heavy metal rod everywhere, learners can construct their own flexible one-metre measuring ropes. Creating a metre rope is a wonderful hands-on practical activity. First, take a piece of sturdy cotton rope or thick twine. Place one end of the rope against the zero mark of a standard metal metre ruler or tailor measuring tape. Tie a neat, firm knot right at the very beginning to establish your starting zero point. Next, stretch the rope carefully along the entire length of the metre ruler, making sure it lies completely straight without any slack, sagging curves, or accidental twists. When you reach the one hundred centimetre mark at the far end of the ruler, tie a second firm knot. The distance between the inner edges of these two knots is now precisely one metre long. Trim away any excess cord beyond the knot. This lightweight knotted rope serves as an exceptionally handy portable tool. It can bend around curved garden beds, stretch along uneven floors, and measure classroom furniture quickly and accurately without scratching polished surfaces. With two simple knots, a plain piece of twine transforms into an empowering scientific instrument of discovery.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many centimetres are there between the two knots of a 1-metre measuring rope?', 'options': ['100 centimetres', '10 centimetres', '50 centimetres', '1,000 centimetres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'One standard metre equals one hundred centimetres.', 'explanation': 'By definition in the metric system, 1 metre is exactly equal to 100 centimetres.'}, {'question': 'Why is a knotted measuring rope especially useful compared to a rigid wooden stick?', 'options': ['It is lightweight and can bend easily around curved or irregular surfaces', 'It can be eaten as a snack', 'It changes its length whenever it gets hot', 'It is completely invisible'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Flexibility allows ropes to follow contours and wrap around objects.', 'explanation': "A rope's flexibility allows it to follow curved shapes and store easily in a pocket or bag."}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Wall Height Sorting', 'subtitle': 'Categorizing student standing heights against a permanent one-metre wall benchmark', 'speech': 'A fantastic way to understand the physical scale of one metre is to mark a permanent reference line on the classroom wall. Using a rigid measuring rod held vertically from the flat floor, a clear horizontal line is drawn with colourful chalk at exactly one metre height. Students take turns standing straight against the wall with their backs flat and heels touching the floor. A wooden ruler placed horizontally across the crown of the head indicates whether the child stands above, below, or right on the one-metre line. In a typical primary grade class, learners discover exciting differences in their physical growth. Some children have standing heights that reach well over one metre, measuring perhaps one hundred and fifteen or one hundred and twenty centimetres. Other younger companions stand slightly below the one-metre chalk mark. Grouping friends into two categories—those taller than one metre and those shorter than one metre—turns abstract numbers into a vivid physical experience. It also provides a memorable visual anchor, allowing children to estimate everyday heights in their surroundings by simply comparing them to their own body standing heights. Marking growth over the academic year inspires pride and reinforces the real-world value of standard metric benchmarks.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If a child stands with the top of their head at 115 cm, how does their height compare to 1 metre?', 'options': ['More than 1 metre (15 cm taller than 100 cm)', 'Less than 1 metre', 'Exactly 1 metre', 'Zero metres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '1 metre is 100 cm. Compare 115 cm with 100 cm.', 'explanation': 'Because 115 cm is greater than 100 cm (1 m), the child is taller than 1 metre by 15 cm.'}, {'question': 'Where must a vertical measuring rod start when marking a 1-metre height on the wall?', 'options': ['From the zero mark resting flat on the floor level', 'From the middle of the wall near the window', 'From the ceiling pointing downward', 'From a desktop held above the floor'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Standing height is measured from the baseline where feet rest.', 'explanation': 'The baseline for vertical height measurement must begin at the floor level where heels touch.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Everyday Metre Benchmark', 'subtitle': 'Classifying environmental objects into lengths less than, equal to, or greater than one metre', 'speech': 'Once learners develop a clear mental picture of one metre, they can classify familiar objects in their environment into three distinct size categories. These categories are: lengths less than one metre, lengths approximately equal to one metre, and lengths greater than one metre. Consider small personal items like a wooden pencil, a reading notebook, an eraser, a spoon, or a water bottle. All of these items measure significantly less than one hundred centimetres, placing them firmly in the less than one metre group. Next, look for objects that closely approximate a single metre. The height of an ordinary school study desk, the length of an acoustic guitar, a standard cricket bat, or the waist height of an adult teacher are all remarkably close to one metre long. Finally, observe large architectural and transport features. The height of the classroom entrance door, the length of the green chalkboard, a teacher desk, a family car, or a large passenger bus are all substantially longer than one metre. Sorting objects into these three benchmark ranges trains your eyes to perform rapid, confident real-world estimations without needing an immediate measuring tape. Mental benchmarks give learners an intuitive frame of reference for judging distances in everyday life.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following common objects typically has a length of less than 1 metre?', 'options': ['A student geometry pencil', 'A school bus', 'A tall classroom door', 'A railway train carriage'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think about which object easily fits inside a small pencil pouch.', 'explanation': 'A pencil measures around 15 to 20 centimetres, which is much less than 1 metre (100 cm).'}, {'question': 'Which everyday classroom fixture is usually greater than 1 metre in height?', 'options': ['The classroom entrance door', 'A wooden chalkboard eraser', 'A stick of chalk', 'A student sharpener'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of an architectural structure that adults can walk through standing up.', 'explanation': 'A classroom door is typically around 2 metres tall, making it significantly greater than 1 metre.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Folding Half Metres', 'subtitle': 'Partitioning one standard metre into two equal halves of fifty centimetres', 'speech': 'A metre is a generous unit of length, but many interesting objects around us are smaller than a full metre yet larger than a tiny finger width. To measure these intermediate lengths conveniently, we can partition our one-metre rope into fractions. Take your knotted one-metre rope and bring the two endpoint knots together so they touch exactly. Smooth down the folded cord and press the opposite looped end to form a crisp midpoint crease. By folding one whole metre into two congruent parts, each half represents one-half of a metre, written mathematically as one over two metres. Because one full metre contains one hundred centimetres, dividing that distance into two equal portions yields fifty centimetres for each half. Therefore, one-half metre is exactly fifty centimetres long. If you place two half-metre ropes end to end, fifty centimetres plus fifty centimetres equals one hundred centimetres, perfectly restoring the whole metre. Many household items, such as a large storage jar, a computer monitor screen, or the width of a classroom chair, match this handy half-metre benchmark. Folding standard lengths unlocks an intuitive understanding of fractional linear measurements. This practical folding technique bridges the gap between hands-on physical manipulation and formal fractional arithmetic concepts.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many centimetres are there in one-half of a metre (1/2 m)?', 'options': ['50 centimetres', '25 centimetres', '75 centimetres', '10 centimetres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide 100 centimetres by 2 equal parts.', 'explanation': 'Half of 100 centimetres is 50 centimetres (100 ÷ 2 = 50 cm).'}, {'question': 'How many half-metre lengths are required to make one whole metre?', 'options': ['Exactly 2 half-metre lengths', '4 half-metre lengths', '10 half-metre lengths', '1 half-metre length'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Two halves always combine to make one complete whole.', 'explanation': 'Two halves make a whole: 50 cm + 50 cm = 100 cm = 1 metre.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Quarter Metre Creases', 'subtitle': 'Discovering twenty-five centimetre quarters and three-quarter metre combinations', 'speech': 'We can extend our paper and rope folding discoveries even further to create smaller standard fractions. Start with your folded half-metre rope and fold it in half a second time, bringing the folded loop to meet the two outer knots. When you unfold the rope completely, you will find three distinct fold creases dividing the entire cord into four equal segments. Each of these four equal pieces represents one-quarter of a metre, written symbolically as one over four metres. Since a full metre contains one hundred centimetres, dividing one hundred by four equal parts gives exactly twenty-five centimetres for each quarter. This means one-quarter metre equals twenty-five centimetres, two-quarters equal fifty centimetres or one-half metre, and three-quarters equal seventy-five centimetres. If you hold three segments together, twenty-five plus twenty-five plus twenty-five gives seventy-five centimetres, known as three-quarters of a metre. Having knots or marks at twenty-five, fifty, and seventy-five centimetres turns your humble piece of rope into a versatile ruler. Learners can now measure smaller party items, such as gift boxes, decorative paper masks, and flower bouquets, with impressive precision. Understanding how quarters accumulate to build half and three-quarter lengths empowers young minds with rich proportional reasoning capabilities.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the length of one-quarter of a metre (1/4 m) in centimetres?', 'options': ['25 centimetres', '50 centimetres', '75 centimetres', '20 centimetres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide 100 centimetres into 4 equal pieces.', 'explanation': '100 cm divided by 4 equals 25 cm. Thus, 1/4 m = 25 cm.'}, {'question': 'How many centimetres equal three-quarters of a metre (3/4 m)?', 'options': ['75 centimetres', '25 centimetres', '50 centimetres', '100 centimetres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Three quarters means 25 cm + 25 cm + 25 cm.', 'explanation': 'Three quarters of 100 cm is 3 × 25 cm = 75 cm.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Object Fraction Checklist', 'subtitle': 'Testing classroom furniture against fractional quarter, half, and whole metre cords', 'speech': 'With fractional measuring cords prepared, children can conduct an investigative classroom measurement survey. Equipped with their knotted ropes marked at quarter-metre, half-metre, and full-metre intervals, students test diverse objects and record their findings in a structured classification table. For each tested object, the students place the cord along its main edge and check which benchmark it matches most closely. For example, testing a standard wooden ruler reveals that it measures thirty centimetres, which is slightly more than a quarter-metre but less than a half-metre. Testing a student backpack height might show that it aligns almost perfectly with the fifty-centimetre half-metre knot. A classroom window pane might measure seventy-five centimetres wide, exactly matching the three-quarter mark. Meanwhile, the teacher bookshelf ledge might measure two full metres, matching two entire rope lengths laid consecutively end to end. By logging these physical comparisons systematically in their math notebooks, learners connect abstract fraction symbols directly to tangible physical dimensions. They learn to view the surrounding world through an observant mathematical lens, appreciating both whole units and fractional parts. Systematic data collection transforms ordinary classroom exploration into authentic scientific inquiry, turning abstract fractions into tangible, measurable reality through collaborative observation and disciplined recording.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A classroom backpack measures 48 cm tall. Which benchmark is it closest to?', 'options': ['Half-metre (50 cm)', 'Quarter-metre (25 cm)', 'Full metre (100 cm)', 'Two metres (200 cm)'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '48 cm is only 2 cm away from 50 cm.', 'explanation': '48 cm is extremely close to 50 cm (1/2 metre), differing by just 2 centimetres.'}, {'question': 'If a bookshelf measures 200 centimetres long, how many 1-metre ropes fit along its edge?', 'options': ['Exactly 2 ropes', '1 rope', '4 ropes', '10 ropes'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Each rope is 100 cm. How many 100s are in 200?', 'explanation': '200 cm ÷ 100 cm/rope = 2 complete metre ropes.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Jump Throw Benchmarks', 'subtitle': 'Measuring standing broad jumps and softball throws on the outdoor playground', 'speech': "Measurement activities become even more thrilling when moved outside to the school playground. Here, students test athletic performances such as standing long jumps and softball throws against metre benchmarks. To set up the jumping track, a starting takeoff line is drawn with white chalk. Then, using their one-metre ropes, learners mark lines at one metre, one and a half metres, two metres, and two and a half metres along the sand pit or turf. Each jumper stands with toes behind the start line, swings their arms, and leaps forward with both feet. The jump distance is measured from the starting line to the closest landing point made by the jumper's heels. A young jumper might land at one metre and twenty centimetres, which is more than one metre but less than one and a half metres. For softball throws, the throwing line serves as the origin, and distances often reach five, eight, or ten metres. Measuring athletic jumps and throws connects physical movement, personal growth, and sports statistics to active mathematical measurement, reinforcing that distance is a continuous dimension spanning both small increments and expansive fields. Experiencing distances in physical motion grounds mathematical understanding in joyful personal achievement and spatial mastery.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A student jumps and lands at 135 cm from the takeoff line. Which interval describes this jump?', 'options': ['Between 1 metre (100 cm) and 1.5 metres (150 cm)', 'Less than 1 metre', 'More than 2 metres', 'Exactly 2 metres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '135 cm is greater than 100 cm and less than 150 cm.', 'explanation': '135 cm lies squarely between 100 cm (1 m) and 150 cm (1.5 m).'}, {'question': "Where should a long jump be measured from on the jumper's body upon landing?", 'options': ['The heel mark closest to the starting takeoff line', 'The furthest fingertip reaching forward', "The top of the jumper's head", 'The front toes of the leading foot'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Standard athletic rules measure from the point of contact nearest to the starting line.', 'explanation': 'In long jump measurement, distance is taken from the starting line to the nearest point of contact made by the heels.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Rope Cut Calculations', 'subtitle': 'Solving practical rope cutting, footstep paces, and room perimeter puzzles', 'speech': "To wrap up our measurement unit, learners tackle three intriguing real-world calculation puzzles. The first puzzle involves dividing a rope. Suppose you have a long rope and wish to cut it into four equal pieces for a team game. How many scissor cuts must you make? While many guess four cuts, the correct answer is only three cuts. Each straight cut through a single cord creates one additional segment, so making three cuts divides the rope into four equal pieces. The second puzzle explores pacing. If a child's natural walking footstep covers roughly fifty centimetres, how many footsteps does it take to walk one metre? Since one metre is one hundred centimetres, fifty plus fifty equals one hundred, meaning exactly two footsteps cover one metre. Finally, students calculate classroom wall perimeter. If the front wall is eight metres long and the side wall is six metres long, the four perimeter walls together equal eight plus eight plus six plus six, totaling twenty-eight metres. These concluding puzzles illustrate how arithmetic operations of addition, multiplication, and spatial logic combine to solve everyday practical measurement challenges with ease and confidence. Mathematical problem solving brings together geometric observation, arithmetic reasoning, and real-world practical mastery.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many scissor cuts are needed to divide a single long rope into 4 equal pieces?', 'options': ['3 cuts', '4 cuts', '2 cuts', '5 cuts'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Each cut produces one additional piece. Think of cutting a ribbon once to get 2 pieces.', 'explanation': 'Making 1 cut produces 2 pieces, 2 cuts produce 3 pieces, and 3 cuts produce 4 pieces.'}, {'question': 'If each natural step is 50 cm, how many steps are needed to pace across a 4-metre wide room?', 'options': ['8 steps (2 steps per metre × 4 metres)', '4 steps', '10 steps', '16 steps'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Each metre takes 2 steps. Multiply 2 steps by 4 metres.', 'explanation': 'Since 1 metre = 2 steps (50 cm + 50 cm), a 4-metre room takes 4 × 2 = 8 steps.'}]}]

